.TH MKPK-PROVISION 1 "2026-08-26" "mkpk-provision 0.8.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mkpk-provision \- provision and deploy the MikroTik PSK port-knock layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkpk-provision
.I COMMAND
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkpk-provision
is the administrator tool. It manages a single YAML config describing one or
more routers, their services and the users allowed to reach them, renders the
RouterOS script, deploys it over SSH, and hands each user a compact invite blob.
It is a complete command-line tool suitable for scripting and automation
(e.g. Ansible); the local web UI
.RB ( serve )
and the desktop app
.RB ( mkpk-provision-desktop )
are optional front-ends over the same core and add nothing the CLI cannot do.
.PP
The config defaults to
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mkpk/mkpk.yaml
(else
.IR ~/.config/mkpk/mkpk.yaml );
override it with
.B --config
on any command. It holds every secret (PSKs, SSH key/password) and is written
0600 with an atomic replace.
.PP
By default the config stays on the operator's machine and
.B serve
listens on loopback without a password. A team can instead run one shared
instance behind a reverse proxy: setting an admin password turns on sessions
and network access \(em see
.B serve
below and the deployment guide in the project documentation.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B secret generate \fR[\fB--bytes\fR \fIN\fR]
Print a fresh base64url-safe secret.
.TP
.B profile init --out \fIFILE\fB --router-name \fINAME\fB --router-address \fIHOST\fR
Create a starter config with a generated PSK and safe defaults.
.TP
.B config validate --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--json\fR]
Load, apply defaults, check invariants and print a secret-free summary. With
.B --json
the summary is emitted as JSON for automation.
.TP
.B router set --config \fIFILE\fB --name \fINAME\fB --address \fIHOST\fR \
[\fB--ssh-user\fR \fIU\fR] [\fB--ssh-key\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB--ssh-agent\fR] [\fB--ssh-port\fR \fIN\fR]
Create or update a router. --address is the public address end users knock;
SSH deploy uses it unless a separate deploy address is set.
.TP
.B router remove --config \fIFILE\fB --name \fINAME\fR
Remove a router and its services.
.TP
.B service add --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] --name \fINAME\fR \
--stage1-port \fIN\fB --stage2-port \fIN\fB --token-port \fIN\fR \
--target-type \fIforward|local\fB --target-port \fIN\fR \
[\fB--target-to-address\fR \fIIP\fR] [\fB--target-to-port\fR \fIN\fR]
Add a service. A forward target dst-nats to an internal host:port; a local
target gates a port on the router itself.
.TP
.B user add --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] --name \fINAME\fB --services \fIa,b\fR
Grant a user access to the listed services on a router (generates a PSK if none).
.TP
.B user remove --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] --name \fINAME\fR [\fB--all-routers\fR]
Revoke access on a router, or everywhere with --all-routers.
.TP
.B token --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] --client \fINAME\fR [\fB--service\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB--bucket\fR \fIN\fR]
Print the current time token for a (user, router, service) \(em for debugging.
.TP
.B routeros render --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB--out\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Render the RouterOS .rsc for a router (all services and users).
.TP
.B export --config \fIFILE\fB --user \fINAME\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB--out\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Produce a user's invite blob. All the user's routers when --router is omitted.
.TP
.B deploy \fR[\fBstatus\fR|\fBuninstall\fR] \fB--config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--router\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB--json\fR]
Install/update the mkpk layer over SSH (default), report install state
.RB ( status ),
or remove it
.RB ( uninstall ).
Connection credentials come from the router. With
.B --json
the result (action, hashes, applied) is emitted as JSON for automation.
.TP
.B serve --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--addr\fR \fI127.0.0.1:8765\fR] [\fB--behind-proxy\fR]
Start the web UI. With no admin password it is the local tool: loopback only,
gated by a per-session token. When an admin password exists (see
.BR passwd ),
the UI requires a login, keeps server-side sessions and can listen on a
network address.
.IP
Two refusals protect the shared mode, because this config grants SSH access to
every router: a non-loopback address without an admin password is refused, and
so is plain HTTP on a non-loopback address without
.B --behind-proxy
(or
.BR MKPK_BEHIND_PROXY=1 ),
which asserts that a reverse proxy terminates TLS in front.
.TP
.B passwd --config \fIFILE\fR [\fB--password\fR \fIPW\fR]
Set or replace the admin password for the networked UI. Stored as an argon2id
record in
.I mkpk-admin.json
beside the config; read from standard input when
.B --password
is omitted.
.TP
.B version
Print the program version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mkpk/mkpk.yaml
Default config path (0600). Holds all secrets.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mkpk/mkpk-admin.json
Admin password record (argon2id, 0600), created beside the config when a
password is set. Deliberately separate from the config, which gets copied and
diffed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MKPK_ADMIN_PASSWORD
Seeds the admin password on first start when no record exists yet. An existing
record always wins, so a stale value cannot reset the password.
.TP
.B MKPK_BEHIND_PROXY
Same as
.BR --behind-proxy .
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize, add a service and a user, deploy, and export an invite:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mkpk-provision profile init --out mkpk.yaml --router-name r1 --router-address r1.example.com
mkpk-provision service add --config mkpk.yaml --name ssh \\
  --stage1-port 41011 --stage2-port 41012 --token-port 41013 \\
  --target-type forward --target-port 22 --target-to-address 192.0.2.10 --target-to-port 22
mkpk-provision user add --config mkpk.yaml --name laptop --services ssh
mkpk-provision deploy --config mkpk.yaml
mkpk-provision export --config mkpk.yaml --user laptop --out laptop.mkpk
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkpk (1)
.SH PROJECT
https://github.com/LazyGatto/mikrotik-psk-knock
